{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And the camps that camp southward shall set out</b> – Scripture explains two תרועות (blasts): the first for the journeying of the camps of the east (Numbers 10:5), and the second for the journeying of the camps of the south. And it says <b>teru'ah they shall blow for their journeys</b> – that they shall blow thus for all their journeys, meaning that they shall blow a third teru'ah for the journeying of the camps camping westward, and a fourth for the journeying of those camping northward, for each division journeys independently, as it will explain: \"and the standard of the camp of the children of Ephra shall set out\" (Numbers 10:22), \"and the standard of the camp of the children of Dan shall set out\" (Numbers 10:25). And so it is stated in the Baraita of the Work of the Tabernacle (chapter 13). But in the Sifrei (Beha'alotkha 73) they taught: Just as one blows for the east and for the south, so does one blow for the west and for the north? Scripture says: <b>teru'ah they shall blow for their journeys</b> – one teki'ah for both of them. And some say: three for every direction. End quote. And so indeed is the plain sense of Scripture, that they blow for every direction as I have explained:" "<b>teru'ah they shall blow for their journeys</b> – I have already explained in the section of \"Speak to the priests\" (Leviticus 23:24) that the teru'ah alludes to the attribute of judgment, for so it is written concerning the journeys: \"at the mouth of HaShem, by the hand of Moses\" (Numbers 9:23), and it is that which defeats in battle. And it is written: \"And when you go to war in your land… and you shall blow\" (Numbers 10:9), and therefore Moses said: \"and may Your enemies flee before You\" (Numbers 10:35). And I have already explained the secret of the faces in the Decalogue (Exodus 20:3). So too you will see that the wall of Jericho fell by a teru'ah, as it is written: \"until the day I say to you: 'Blow!' and you shall blow\" (Joshua 6:10), and it is written: \"and the people blew a great teru'ah and the wall fell\" (ibid. v. 20), and therefore it was a חרם (a ban-devoted thing). And when assembling the congregation, teki'ot are fitting, for the peshutah (a plain sustained note) alludes to the attribute of mercy, for His right hand is stretched out to receive the penitent. Therefore it says when it rests: \"Return, O HaShem, to the myriads of thousands of Israel\" (Numbers 10:36). And it is written: \"And on the day of your rejoicing and on your appointed seasons, you shall blow\" (Numbers 10:10), for war pertains to teru'ah and the festivals and rejoicing pertain to mercy. And our Rabbis (Rosh HaShanah 33b) transmitted: a peshutah before it and a peshutah after it, and a teru'ah in the middle, so as not to cut short the plantings on Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur and concerning the offering. But the intention here is this for teki'ah and that for teru'ah, and המשכיל יבין:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The Sifrei passage ends with the phrase עד כאן ('end quote'), which I have rendered explicitly to mark where Ramban's citation of the midrash concludes.", 1 "The phrase על הקרבן ('concerning the offering') refers to the trumpeting over the sacrificial service; I have rendered it literally to preserve Ramban's compressed reference without expanding it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:13.085468543Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}